ss well than  the first group simply because I don't  have as big a vocabulary I haven't done  as much listening  in them and I make more mistakes but I  can converse comfortably but with more  mistakes  then comes probably Italian where I have  traveled to Italy and so I can speak  that language  and then probably Cantonese and then  comes Portuguese which is very similar  to Spanish is almost like Spanish spoken  with a few different words and different  pronunciation but I've had less  opportunity to speak it some while in  Brazil some well in Portugal but really  not that much so that my Portuguese I  don't feel as confident it ends up being  a conscious effort to convert my Spanish  into Portuguese and I don't feel so  comfortable speaking it next comes  Russian where I have had a lot of input  lots of listening and reading some  opportunity to speak I was in Russia  briefly for a weaker 12 days back seven  or eight years ago and more recently I  was in Ukraine and particularly in Kiev  and eastern Ukraine  I was speaking Russian most of the time  but I can turn it on with mistakes but I  can turn it on  same with Ukrainian now which I learned  after Russian and I was in fact on  television in Ukraine in Kiev primetime  television speaking both Russian and  Ukrainian I understand the languages  well I speak Russian I understand  Russian much better but I understand  them both comfortably and I can speak  albeit with mistakes so that takes me I  believe unless I've forgotten the  language in there that should bring me  up to both 12 that I can say I speak  comfortably these are the languages that  I speak next comes a group of languages  where I have hardly had any practice  speaking but I have a fair degree of  understanding so these are languages  that I would say that I know but I don't  speak but it wouldn't take me more than  I mean Korean is saying next right and I  according to link I have 40,000 words  that I know in Korean but and when I was  in Seoul and I gathered with a group of  people there we had dinner and I was  speaking Korea but I haven't spoken much  Korean I've had a few isolated  conversations on  lying conversations I've worked at it  quite a bit but I don't feel comfortable  in saying that I speak it although you  know superficially it can sound as if as  if I speak it so but it wouldn't take me  more than a couple of days to get I  believe in a Korean environment to get  my Korean up to the level where I could  comfortably say I speak it and then I go  check polish you know Slovak that are  related languages here again I  understand them a lot of them anyway and  given an opportunity to be in that  environment I can elevate them to the  level of languages that I speak but I  don't like to say I speak them I'd like  to say I know them I know of them I'm  acquainted with them but I can't say  that I comfortably speak them so now  we've got the first twelve are the ones  that I speak the next four are ones that  I think I could you know I'm close to  being able to say I speak but I'm not  quite there yet because I haven't had  enough speaking experience then come the  languages that I've been working on more  recently  first of all remaining and Greek that I  put a fair amount of concentrated effort  into for a few months before going to  those countries and of course Romanian  is much easier than Greek because so  much vocabulary is similar to Italian  some of it is similar to Slavic  languages about 20% of it it's written  in the Latin alphabet and I found that  even after two months of a lot of input  based learning and some online tutoring  I was able to communicate in Romania  Greek I spent eight months at it it's a  different writing system there are fewer  sort of freebie vocabulary items and I  was able to communicate comfortably  while I was on the island of Crete in  both those languages if confronted today  by someone speaking Romanian or Greek I  wouldn't be able to say much if anything  at all so those are certainly not  languages that I speak but there are  like those are languages that I have  invested in and if I were to go back to  them go back to some of the material  I've been using to learn and start  speaking I could  again raise the level raise the level  but there's certainly not languages that  I can claim to speak now come my last  three languages Arabic Persian and  Turkish and they're my level is that  they are no longer unfamiliar to me I  know how they work I have you know in  Persian 2,500 words in Arabic 8,000  words and Turkish 6,000 words in some  ways Persian is the easiest of the three  because the structure is so similar to  European languages but of course the  writing system is a bit of an obstacle  and those are languages that I will be  working on all three of them over the  next year or so to gradually improve my  level will they ever reach the level of  the first 12 I don't think so  unless I spend a lot of time in Turkey  or in Iran or in an arabic-speaking  country and there the problem with  Arabic again is that I'm studying  traditional Arabic and there's no  country where that's the sort of  national language so but who knows maybe  I'll spend some time in you know Lebanon  and learn Levantine Arabic or in Jordan  or somewhere so there you have it it's a  summary of the 12 languages that I can  say that I speak four languages that I  could elevate to the level of languages  that I speak but I just consider them  languages that I know to languages that  I learned over a short period of time  which are at a lower level of  development and then the final three so  the total is 21 but only 12 of them can  I genuinely say that I speak ok thank  you and let me know what you think bye  for now  you
 

